# crush shell config. Linked to ~/.config/crush/crushrc by
# home-manager/modules/tools/crush.nix, which also owns crush.json beside it.
# Keep hooks, global_context_paths, and permissions.allowed_tools out of this
# file: crushrc wins on key conflicts, so setting one here would replace the
# module's value.
#
# crush runs this with its embedded shell at every startup; a non-zero exit
# (e.g. 127 for a missing command) aborts config loading entirely. So nothing
# here may assume a binary exists - crush.nix reaches the Linux devcontainer
# too, which has no envchain.

# Venice is a built-in provider (catwalk's venice.json, generated from
# Venice's /models): base URL, the full model catalog (kimi-k3,
# openai-gpt-6-astra, ...), context windows, and prices all ship with crush,
# and it reads $VENICE_API_KEY on its own. The only gap is that the key lives
# in the macOS keychain, not the environment, so this supplies it from
# envchain. `printenv` yields the bare value; `env | grep` would yield
# VENICE_API_KEY=<key>. Where envchain is absent, an exported
# VENICE_API_KEY still works with no help from this file.
# An empty result (envchain present, no venice namespace) is skipped rather
# than passed on, since --api-key "" would override that env fallback.
if command -v envchain >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  venice_key="$(envchain venice printenv VENICE_API_KEY 2>/dev/null)"
  if [ -n "$venice_key" ]; then
    provider add venice --api-key "$venice_key"
  fi
fi
